    bio: "Arno Peters was born in Berlin in 1916 and trained as a historian, earning his doctorate in 1945 with a thesis on political propaganda in film. He was not a cartographer by profession, and the academic establishment of mapmaking would never quite let him forget it — but it was precisely his outsider status that allowed him to see something the professionals had stopped noticing: that the world\'s most widely used map was, by any honest measure, a political document as much as a geographical one.\n\nIn 1974, Peters unveiled his equal-area cylindrical projection at a press conference in Bonn, presenting it as a corrective to centuries of Eurocentric distortion. The Peters map showed the world\'s landmasses in their correct relative areas, which meant that Africa and South America suddenly loomed vast and central while Europe and North America contracted toward something closer to their true proportions. The presentation was deliberately provocative, and Peters made grand claims — that his map represented a democratic, decolonial vision of the planet — that professional cartographers found both overblown and threatening.\n\nThe backlash from the cartographic community was fierce and, in some respects, justified. Peters' map, while area-accurate, introduced severe shape distortions: equatorial regions appeared compressed while polar regions stretched, and the resulting Africa looked oddly elongated, like a continent that had been stood on its end. Cartographers pointed out, correctly, that earlier equal-area projections — particularly those of Mollweide and Gall — had accomplished much the same thing decades or centuries before, and with less fanfare. Peters was accused of reinventing the wheel and then claiming to have invented the wheel.\n\nBut whatever the merits of the technical dispute, Peters had struck a nerve that proved impossible to ignore. His map was adopted by the United Nations Development Programme, by numerous NGOs, and by school systems in Germany, the Netherlands, and parts of the developing world. The debate he ignited forced a generation of educators, policymakers, and ordinary map-readers to ask a question that had gone unasked for centuries: why do we use the maps we use, and whose interests do those choices serve? Peters died in Bremen in 2002, a controversial figure, but one whose uncomfortable question had permanently changed the terms of the conversation."
